  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a vacuum cleaner suck up dirt?",
      "a": "It does not really suck. The fan pushes air out, lowering the pressure inside, and the higher air pressure outside pushes air and dust in through the nozzle.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes a vacuum cleaner suck?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a bagless vacuum work?",
      "a": "Air full of dust spins round in a cyclone. The heavier dust is thrown outwards against the wall and falls into the bin, while the lighter air escapes through the centre.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a cyclone separate dust?",
        "How does a Dyson work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a vacuum lose suction?",
      "a": "Usually a clogged filter, a full bin or a blocked hose. Anything that stops air flowing reduces the pressure difference that pulls the dust in.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is my vacuum not picking up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a vacuum have a filter if it has a cyclone?",
      "a": "The cyclone removes most dust, but the finest particles can follow the air. The filter catches them so they do not blow back into the room.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a HEPA filter do in a vacuum?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are vacuum cleaners so loud?",
      "a": "The fan spins at tens of thousands of turns a minute, and the fast-moving air rushing through narrow parts makes a lot of noise.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes a vacuum noisy?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "James Dyson built 5,127 prototypes before perfecting his first bagless cyclone vacuum cleaner.",
    "Cyclone separators are also used in factories and sawmills to remove dust from air."
